1
00:00:06,883 --> 00:00:11,868
Frederick Douglass was born into slavery in Maryland around 1818.

2
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:14,238
He never knew his own birthday.

3
00:00:14,351 --> 00:00:21,048
He escaped north in 1838, and in 1845 published the story that made him famous.

4
00:00:21,331 --> 00:00:23,607
Sent to Baltimore at about eight,

5
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:26,849
he began to learn the alphabet from Sophia Auld,

6
00:00:26,849 --> 00:00:28,958
until her husband forbade it.

7
00:00:29,071 --> 00:00:31,463
He bought a schoolbook of speeches,

8
00:00:31,463 --> 00:00:35,552
The Columbian Orator, which shaped his ideas about liberty.

9
00:00:35,969 --> 00:00:38,071
So he kept learning in secret,

10
00:00:38,071 --> 00:00:43,335
trading bread for lessons and copying letters from the timbers of the shipyard.

11
00:00:45,252 --> 00:00:50,036
Hired out to Edward Covey, a farmer known for breaking enslaved people,

12
00:00:50,036 --> 00:00:52,164
he endured months of brutality,

13
00:00:52,164 --> 00:00:53,513
then fought back.

14
00:00:53,796 --> 00:00:57,742
On 3 September 1838, dressed as a sailor,

15
00:00:57,742 --> 00:01:00,282
he left Baltimore by train and ferry,

16
00:01:00,282 --> 00:01:02,387
and reached New York, free.

17
00:01:04,026 --> 00:01:06,424
In New Bedford he took a new name,

18
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:12,545
Douglass, and at Nantucket in August 1841 he spoke to an anti-slavery convention.

19
00:01:12,961 --> 00:01:17,170
The Massachusetts Anti-Slavery Society hired him as a lecturer.

20
00:01:17,453 --> 00:01:20,570
In 1845 he published his Narrative.

21
00:01:20,683 --> 00:01:22,933
The title page said it plainly:

22
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:24,398
written by himself.

23
00:01:24,511 --> 00:01:26,562
It was an immediate success.

24
00:01:26,675 --> 00:01:29,041
He took it to Britain and Ireland,

25
00:01:29,041 --> 00:01:34,496
where supporters raised the money that bought his legal freedom in 1846.

26
00:01:34,849 --> 00:01:39,389
In December 1847 he founded his own newspaper in Rochester,

27
00:01:39,389 --> 00:01:40,622
The North Star.

28
00:01:41,439 --> 00:01:46,726
In July 1848 he spoke at Seneca Falls in support of votes for women.

29
00:01:46,839 --> 00:01:50,503
In 1851 he broke with William Lloyd Garrison,

30
00:01:50,503 --> 00:01:55,414
arguing that the Constitution could be read as an anti-slavery instrument.

31
00:01:55,527 --> 00:02:01,267
He knew John Brown, but in 1859 declined to join the raid on Harpers Ferry,

32
00:02:01,267 --> 00:02:02,674
judging it doomed.

33
00:02:02,957 --> 00:02:07,145
On 5 July 1852 he asked a Rochester audience:

34
00:02:07,145 --> 00:02:10,972
what, to the American slave, is your Fourth of July?

35
00:02:11,255 --> 00:02:16,703
The census counted nearly two and a half million enslaved people in 1840,

36
00:02:16,703 --> 00:02:22,170
over three million in 1850, and almost four million in 1860.

37
00:02:22,523 --> 00:02:25,559
In the Civil War he recruited Black soldiers,

38
00:02:25,559 --> 00:02:28,533
including for the 54th Massachusetts,

39
00:02:28,533 --> 00:02:30,674
where two of his sons served.

40
00:02:30,787 --> 00:02:36,741
In August 1863 he went to the White House to press President Lincoln for equal pay

41
00:02:36,741 --> 00:02:39,140
and protection for Black soldiers.

42
00:02:39,423 --> 00:02:43,219
After the war he campaigned for citizenship and the vote,

43
00:02:43,219 --> 00:02:46,909
and in 1869 supported the Fifteenth Amendment,

44
00:02:46,909 --> 00:02:49,778
even though it did not enfranchise women.

45
00:02:50,131 --> 00:02:55,099
He served as Marshal for the District of Columbia and as minister to Haiti,

46
00:02:55,099 --> 00:02:57,349
and made his home at Cedar Hill,

47
00:02:57,349 --> 00:02:59,014
overlooking Washington.

48
00:02:59,431 --> 00:03:03,789
There he kept writing, telling his own story for a third time.

49
00:03:04,073 --> 00:03:07,009
After his wife Anna died in 1882,

50
00:03:07,009 --> 00:03:09,797
he married Helen Pitts in 1884,

51
00:03:09,797 --> 00:03:12,517
and the marriage drew public criticism.

52
00:03:12,631 --> 00:03:16,597
He died at Cedar Hill on 20 February 1895,

53
00:03:16,597 --> 00:03:20,373
hours after a meeting of the National Council of Women.

54
00:03:20,657 --> 00:03:24,233
If there is no struggle, he said in 1857,

55
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:25,845
there is no progress.
